"\section=1"
"\name=iw4x-steam"
"\summary=start Steam client with IW4x companion"

namespace companion
{
  {
    "<steam-argument>",

    "\h|SYNOPSIS|

     \c{\b{iw4x-steam} [<steam-argument>...]}

     \h|DESCRIPTION|

     The \cb{iw4x-steam} command starts the native Linux Steam client with
     the IW4x companion module preloaded. While the module is loaded, Steam
     friends see IW4x running under Proton as the game being played. The
     arguments are passed to the Steam command unchanged. For example:

     \
     $ iw4x-steam -silent
     \

     The Steam client can only load the module at startup, so it must not
     be running already. If it is, then \cb{iw4x-steam} fails without
     starting anything since \cb{steam} would pass the arguments to the
     running client, which does not load the module.

     The module is preloaded from the \cb{lib/iw4x-steam/} directory of the
     installation that \cb{iw4x-steam} belongs to, in the subdirectory named
     after the dynamic linker's \cb{$PLATFORM} (see \cb{ld.so(8)}). In the
     32-bit Steam client it expands to \cb{i686}, which contains the module.
     In the 64-bit programs that start the client it expands to \cb{x86_64},
     \cb{haswell}, or \cb{xeon_phi}, each of which contains an empty 64-bit
     stand-in that keeps the dynamic linker from warning about the preload.
     The module removes itself from \cb{LD_PRELOAD} as it loads, so the
     processes that these programs start, including games and Proton, do not
     inherit it.

     The companion writes its diagnostics to the Steam client's \cb{stderr},
     which the client normally copies to its console log,
     \cb{~/.steam/steam/logs/console-linux.txt}. Each line starts with
     \cb{[iw4x-steam]} and a successful startup ends with a line like this:

     \
     [iw4x-steam] active (build 2)
     \

     See \l{companion The \cb{companion} Manual} for details on how the
     companion works and on the other diagnostics that it issues.

     \h|ENVIRONMENT|

     \dl|

     \li|\cb{IW4X_STEAM}

     The Steam command to run. If unset, \cb{steam} is searched for in
     \cb{PATH}.|

     \li|\cb{LD_PRELOAD}

     The module is added in front of the existing value, which is otherwise
     preserved.||

     \h|FILES|

     \dl|

     \li|\c{\i{prefix}\b{/lib/iw4x-steam/i686/libiw4x-steam.so}}

     The companion module.|

     \li|\c{\i{prefix}\b{/lib/iw4x-steam/x86_64/libiw4x-steam.so}}

     The 64-bit stand-in. The \cb{haswell} and \cb{xeon_phi} subdirectories
     contain the same stand-in.|

     \li|\c{\b{/dev/shm/iw4x-steam-companion.}\i{uid}}

     The companion status, which the game reads. The companion keeps the
     file locked while it runs, so only one companion is active per user.||

     \h|EXIT STATUS|

     If the module is not installed or Steam is already running, then
     \cb{iw4x-steam} issues diagnostics and exits with status 1. Otherwise,
     it executes the Steam command in its place, so the exit status is that
     of the command.
     "
  }
}
